[{:seon.task/name "a-form-has-a-time-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.turn/act!` evaluates through `seon.repl/eval-string` with no per-form bound, so a model's infinite loop holds its run forever. Correct: a config key bounds a form, passed in act!; the interrupt is the named failure point `seon.intr/run` (docs/failure-points.md), and the interrupted form is recorded."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/an-endless-form-is-interrupted-and-recorded}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-run-past-its-cap-ends"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen in the related A/B (worktree a-text-written-on-a-branch-is-embedded-there, round on-2, 2026-10-25, deepseek, :seon.config.agent/max-turns 30): the run of seon.context/an-unrun-proof-is-unknown-in-the-context recorded 29 screen evals and 36 model evals, no :seon.end fact, status :running and its agent :busy for over an hour after its last turn, with an empty stderr (runs/store-agents/main/a1.err, 0 bytes); its root answered every other request. The round's stats are claude_scratch/related-search/ab-on-2-partial.edn; the store was erased with the round (an experiment store), so what the agent was evaluating is not kept. Not known: whether the last eval hung (a form with no bound, task seon.turn/a-form-has-a-time-bound), the model call never answered (seon.model's edge, no timeout by design: a never-answered call is a recorded stall, AGENTS §9), or the loop missed its cap. The first step reproduces it with the scripted provider at the cap (a reply whose eval never returns; a call never answered) and reads the agent's state over the server while it is busy (seon_agents, the agent's :fx-pending, its last eval record), so the stall is a fact on the run (blocked, stalled) and the run ends rather than holding its agent: never a clock deciding; the fact names what it waits on."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-run-waiting-past-its-cap-says-on-what}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-turn-records-what-it-saw-and-spent"
  :seon.task/plan "A turn records its `:seon.turn/basis` and `:seon.turn/split` (`seon.turn/basis!`), but not the model's raw reply, its finish_reason, model and reasoning, nor the whole split as the agent saw it (4-15 s gaps in a real run had to be traced by hand). Correct: per turn, the messages sent are derivable from the basis; the reply's finish_reason, model and reasoning, and the split context render / model round trip / evals / record / tests are recorded. The dashboard's agent page shows the recorded context, not a second producer of it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/a-turn-records-its-context-reply-and-time-split}} {:seon.task/name "an-outside-context-is-the-turns-context"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.turn/context-block` (the server's read of a task for an outside worktree session) now takes the run's plan and `seon.turn/observe`'s gaps since the branch's merge base (`seon.merge/uncontracted`), as the turn loop does, so main's pre-existing contract gaps are no longer charged to the agent. It still passes turn n of max-turns 1 to `seon.turn/basis!`, so the context says \"turn 1 of 1\" while the run's env says 8. Correct: context-block passes the run's env max-turns, so the outside session reads exactly what the turn loop's model would. Replaces the second set of basis arguments; nothing new."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/an-outside-context-names-only-the-branchs-own-gaps}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-fork-server-hands-main-s-index"
  :seon.task/plan "An agent's first turn listed the reconciler's index itself (`seon.turn/index!` with no index held: `seon.reconcile/index` of the branch's db), every agent once: on a worktree root's main of 908 tasks, 166M ticks and 700 MB with the index's deep contract, 33M and 160 MB with it shallow (the branch jolt/the-index-is-checked-once). The fork server holds main's index as it holds the reach graph: listed when it starts, observed forward at each fork over what differs (```seon.fork-server/index-at-fork```), and handed with the fork request, so the agent's state starts with :index {:db main :index ...} and the first turn observes its branch from it: 2.0M ticks and 10 MB, measured on a fresh run. The fork server pays one observe a fork (main moved by the spawn's commit) and one listing when it starts. Not this task, measured on the same root: a fresh agent's first read still costs about 126M ticks against 33M a later one; its first observe (the test service's plan, seon.problems/caused-split) costs 50M against about 8M warm with the same calls (17 for caused-split both times), and its first render about 40M more than a later one. The same calls costing 6x on the first go is cold reads: seon.db/after-fork! forgets the parent's connections, so the agent's store starts with an empty node cache and decodes every node it reads."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/the-fork-server-step}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-branch-asks-whether-a-test-is-a-process-test"
  :seon.task/plan "Dogfood 0.7, form: (seon.agent/branch-graph) at the REPL is an arity error (it takes db, base db and inputs), and no public my.agent function answers \"is this test a process test on my branch\", which decides whether (my.agent/prove) runs it or defers it. Add that answer to my.agent beside tests-reaching and deferred (status of a test: :process, :plain), reading the reach graph the test plan reads. Proof: (my.agent/tests-reaching ..) rows carry :process? for a test reaching a process var."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/tests-reaching-says-which-tests-are-process-tests}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-failed-outside-run-ends-failed"
  :seon.task/plan "Dogfood 0.7 (found 2026-10-27 through the server): after (my.task/fail! \"why\" evidence) an outside agent's seon_done answers {:status :closed :outcome :green :held {:seon.task/failure ..}}: the run's own end says :green though it failed its task, and only the held failure shows it. Cause: ```seon.turn/hand-back!``` writes its end from the done rule alone (:green when observe has no reasons, else :red) and never reads the stop my.task/fail! requested (```seon.turn/request-stop!```, [:turn :stop]), which the turn loop (```seon.turn/work!```) ends the run :failed on. Correct, at the producer: hand-back! ends the run :failed with the stop's reason when the agent failed its task, as work! does; the choice is one pure function of the stop and the done rule's reasons, which both read. Proof: that function answers [:failed reason] for a :failed stop whatever the reasons, [:green nil] with neither, [:red text] with reasons and no stop. Replaces the second copy of the choice. About 8 lines."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-outside-runs-end-is-failed-when-its-agent-failed-the-task}}]
